c Sea  was heavily mined and teeming with  Russian submarines oh and despite what I  said earlier it was a brutally cold  winter so yeah icebergs were actually a  genuine danger too  all in all the deck was heavily stacked  against the fleeing Germans  and it was to passengers of The Wilhelm  gulshtof that paid the maximum price on  the 30th of January 1945 the port of  gadinia was full of thousands of  refugees including women and children  soldiers Nazi officials and U-Boat  trainees all desperate to get away the  Goldstock was the largest ship available  but it had only been designed to carry  1900 people including its crew  by the time it set sail there were more  than five times that number on board  some people had tickets but most simply  forced their way onto the ship and who  could blame them  when the options are Escape or death  you're going to give escape a go no  matter how dangerous it might seem  according to records there were 6 000  passengers and crew aboard the ship but  the real number is thought to be upwards  of ten thousand ninety percent of whom  were civilians when the ship left Port  just after midday it was hugely  overcrowded with people occupying every  space they could find including the long  empty swimming pool but nobody was  working on their tan records say it was  snowing that day and the Mercury was at  10 degrees below freezing inside the  ship however the sheer number of people  ensured the atmosphere was hot and humid  and many resorted to removing their life  jackets in an attempt to cool down  yeah in hindsight that wasn't ideal but  then again a bit of extra buoyancy  wouldn't have made all that much  difference in the freezing Waters of the  Baltic Sea in what sounds like the start  of a bad joke there were four captains  aboard the ship and they couldn't agree  on the safest route to take  some wanted to stick to the shallower  waters along the coastline to minimize  the chances of encountering submarines  whilst others fought the deeper  mind-swept waters further out to sea  were their best bet  in the end they opted for the latter  at around 6 PM a German mine sweeping  Convoy was spotted in the area and to  avoid any sort of collision one of the  captains ordered at the goldstev's red  and green navigation lights be turned on  lighting the ship up like a floating  Christmas Tree by 7 PM the gold stuff  was being tailed by a Soviet submarine  called s13  even though a torpedo boat was  accompanying the Gustav to protect her  against Subs its submarine detector and  anti-aircraft guns had been frozen  leaving the vessel totally unprotected  s13 reeled in the gulstuff during a  two-hour Chase before firing four  Torpedoes there are rumors that each of  the Torpedoes had been adorned with a  hand-painted message and even though the  fourth missile got wedged in the  submarine's torpedo tube the other three  successfully delivered their mail  within an hour the goldstuff had sunk  dozens of people were killed instantly  by the blasts and thousands more were  drowned trapped below the decks those  who made for the lifeboats had to  navigate a Stampede in which panicked  passengers were crushed to death  all but nine of the lifeboats were  frozen in place and even those that were  broken free were difficult to release  because one of the Torpedoes had hit the  cruise quarters killing the only people  who knew how to operate them  still several lifeboats did start  getting lower towards the sea but about  20 minutes after the first torpedo found  its Mark the ship suddenly listed  violently to Port  the lifeboats that were being glued on  the opposite side of the ship were  smashed into the Gustav's Hull  destroying them and casting their  occupants into the freezing Waters below  the few lifeboats that did make it down  safely were soon faced with a new threat  boarding from the hundreds of swimmers  already in the water  eyewitness accounts speak of desperate  men and women trying to haul themselves  aboard the lifeboats whilst the current  occupants beat them back with fists ores  and some Choice German swear words  support boats did eventually arrive and  between them they were able to rescue  about 1 250 people through the night  they were very very lucky ones  nobody knows exactly how many people  died that night but most estimates put  the figure at around 9 400 poor souls  that's more than six Titanics or three  September 11th  the last survivor was found at dawn when  a German naval officer climbed into a  drifting Lifeboat to check for signs of  Life at first he found none but amongst  the bodies was a baby that had been  tightly wrapped in blankets  it was still alive  the same officer will go on to adopt and  raise the child as his very own in what  was one of the few tiny bright points on  one of the blackest nights in Maritime  history  [Music]  thanks for watching
 

